description:
crate-jmap-cid-types/src/digest.rs:168-173 implements:

    impl TryFrom<String> for Sha256 {
        type Error = Sha256DigestError;
        fn try_from(s: String) -> Result<Self, Self::Error> {
            Self::from_hex($;s)
        }
    }

The TryFrom<String> impl receives an owned String, immediately borrows it as &str for from_hex (line 171), then from_hex itself calls s.to_owned() at line 127 to clone the validated bytes into the Sha256. The original owned String is dropped at end-of-scope. Net effect: every TryFrom<String> call allocates a fresh String of identical contents and discards the input.

This hits twice on the hot path: #[serde(try_from = "String")] on the type (line 100) means every deserialize call routes through this impl. serde_json::from_str allocates a String for the bare-hex wire value, hands it to TryFrom<String>, which then allocates a second String via to_owned. Two heap allocations per deserialize where one would do.

The idiomatic Rust pattern when you receive an owned value is to MOVE it into the result, not to re-clone it. The fix is a small refactor:

    impl Sha256 {
        fn validate(s: &str) -> Result<(), Sha256DigestError> {
            let bytes = s.as_bytes();
            if bytes.len() != 64 { return Err(...); }
            for (i, b) in bytes.iter().enumerate() { ... }
            Ok(())
        }
        pub fn from_hex(s: &str) -> Result<Self, Sha256DigestError> {
            Self::validate(s)?;
            Ok(Self(s.to_owned()))
        }
    }

    impl TryFrom<String> for Sha256 {
        type Error = Sha256DigestError;
        fn try_from(s: String) -> Result<Self, Self::Error> {
            Self::validate(&s)?;
            Ok(Self(s))   // move, not clone
        }
    }

What goes wrong by leaving as-is:
- Doubled allocation on every deserialize. For a wire-format type that's deserialised on every JMAP Blob upload response, this is a measurable cost path, not a microbenchmark curiosity.
- The 'why is TryFrom<String> taking an owned String' question reads as a mistake to a fresh Rust reviewer. The signature promises 'I will consume your String'; the impl breaks that promise by re-cloning.
- Composes with finding .18 (inner [u8;32] repr): under the [u8;32] alternative, the String is dropped after decoding into bytes and the clone disappears for free. But independent of .18, the String-repr path should still move, not clone.

Pre-v0.1.0, the fix is a one-paragraph internal refactor with no public-surface change.
